}],"url":"\/documentation\/readium\/readium-lcp","identifier":"doc:\/\/Readium\/documentation\/Readium\/Readium-LCP","title":"Supporting Readium LCP","role":"article","kind":"article"},"doc://Readium/documentation/Readium/Migration-Guide":{"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/Readium\/documentation\/Readium\/Migration-Guide","title":"Migration Guide","kind":"article","url":"\/documentation\/readium\/migration-guide","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"All migration steps necessary in reading apps to upgrade to major versions of the Swift Readium toolkit will be documented in this file."}],"role":"article"},"doc://Readium/documentation/Readium/Input":{"kind":"article","type":"topic","title":"Observing user input","url":"\/documentation\/readium\/input","role":"article","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"In visual publications like EPUB or PDF, users can interact with the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"VisualNavigator"},{"type":"text","text":" instance using gestures, a keyboard, a mouse, a pencil, or a trackpad. When the publication does not override user input events, you may want to intercept them to trigger actions in your user interface. For example, you can turn pages or toggle the navigation bar on taps, or open a bookmarks screen when pressing the Command-Shift-D hotkey."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Readium\/documentation\/Readium\/Input"},"doc://Readium/documentation/Readium/Highlights":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Readium\/documentation\/Readium\/Highlights","type":"topic","kind":"article","title":"Implementing Highlights","role":"article","url":"\/documentation\/readium\/highlights","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Highlighting lets users mark up passages in a publication for later reference - a core feature of any reading app. In Readium, highlights are built on top of the "},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Decoration API"}],"type":"strong"},{"type":"text","text":". If you want to understand that API in depth or build a custom decoration style, see the "},